    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe exception class, the `ExceptionControllerAdvice` handler, and the placement of a private `rejectWhenCityAtCapacity` helper called at the top of `createOwner` all follow this codebase's established patterns cleanly (mirrors `DuplicateHouseholdException` / `assignHousehold`). One structural issue, though:\n\n**Duplicated \"count owners in this city\" logic.** `rejectWhenCityAtCapacity` (ClinicServiceImpl.java:333\u2013336) computes:\n\n```java\nString city = IdentityNormalizer.normalize(owner.getCity());\nlong cityOwners = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n    .filter(existing -> city.equals(IdentityNormalizer.normalize(existing.getCity())))\n    .count();\n```\n\nwhich is character-for-character the same query/predicate already present in `nextCustomerCode` (lines 353\u2013356), where it's used as `... .count() + 1L`. The same concept \u2014 \"number of owners already in the owner's city\" \u2014 is now expressed in two adjacent methods in the same class.\n\nExtract a single private helper, e.g.:\n\n```java\nprivate long countOwnersInCity(Owner owner) {\n    String city = IdentityNormalizer.normalize(owner.getCity());\n    return ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n        .filter(existing -> city.equals(IdentityNormalizer.normalize(existing.getCity())))\n        .count();\n}\n```\n\nand have both `rejectWhenCityAtCapacity` (`if (countOwnersInCity(owner) >= CITY_CAPACITY)`) and `nextCustomerCode` (`countOwnersInCity(owner) + 1L`) call it. This removes the duplication, keeps the normalization rule defined once, and reads naturally alongside the existing `countNamesakes` helper.\n\n(Minor, optional: the `CITY_CAPACITY` constant is declared in the middle of the class body rather than grouped with the field declarations at the top; co-locating it with the helper is defensible, so not blocking.)",
